									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/arlin-bradley" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7054268); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7054268&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606333236" alt="Arlin Bradley" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7054268)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/arlin-bradley" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7054268); return false;" >Arlin Bradley</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606332969 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Nov 2020 19:36</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13877672/the-tainted-saint-collection-seeking-greenlight">The Tainted Saint Collection (Seeking Greenlight)</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4828046">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Horror</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> Standard</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> SCP-XXXX is a Euclid-class collection of parasitic donor organs that are capable of osmotic extracellular digestion. Individual &quot;fragments&quot; of SCP-XXXX possess passive, non-lethal cognitohazardous properties.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The Tainted Saint Collection is the first entry in a wider narrative I have planned. It's centered around the research of Dr. Lee Elliot, an ex-field agent with an interest in SCPs that can manipulate organic matter. I intend for this narrative to begin (temporally) with a different SCP, an incident report, and a Tale. However, this SCP seemed like a better place to start in practice since it's easier to pitch.</p>
<p>The report starts out normal, with little off-kilter details sprinkled in to breed unease. Note, I described SCP-XXXX as a collection of donor organs in the intro here, but in the article itself that information will not be revealed until the description section. By the time the reader reaches that point, I want them to be hyperaware of the danger associated with even brief contact so the reveal that they were implanted into people hits as hard as possible.</p>
<p>Beyond that, the bulk of the article's narrative will follow the background of each fragment individually, outlining their quirks. SCP-XXXX-B, for example, kills its hosts within a few days, whereas SCP-XXXX-A remained inside the same host for six months. At the end, I plan on including a brief interview between SCP-XXXX-A's ex-host and Dr. Elliot. Lastly, I'll include a short lab report detailing a non-lethal vivisection of SCP-XXXX-A that ended up costing Dr. Elliot his left eye. Spoiler alert: fragments of SCP-XXXX are immobile by choice, not biology.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention Grabber:</strong> They're carnivorous donor organs with cognitohazardous properties. I know I'd read an article about that.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I have way too much experience writing formal academic prose, so my main concern is getting the lore/terminology right. I'm also wondering if anyone knows some good SCPs that introduce recurring researcher characters without making that introduction too intrusive. I don't want the article to end up feeling like it's about Dr. Elliot.</p>

							</div>

								
					
